  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe unraveling of Edouard's remnants continues Thursday into \nFriday with the lowest portions of the complex shearing apart \namong the mean southerlies, while the circulation in the mid- \nlevels lags back to the south and broadens over Central TX. This \ntransition could give us our best rain chance days Thursday into \nFriday with the upper ridge aloft precluding much activity from \nhappening overnight. Pooled Pwat values over 2 inches expand down \ntoward San Antonio late Thursday into Friday to get a few more \nareas a shot of diurnal convection. The latest GFS runs are \ntrending wetter with a 500 mb weakness hanging around Central TX. \nThe more preferred solution among deterministic trends is the \nECMWF which fits in better with the model blends, but there could \nbe an increasing inverted trough extending to connect weak TUTT \nupstream over the Gulf by Friday. Thus expect to see a \ncontinuation of diurnal storms over the Coastal Prairies, but \nperhaps less activity reaching the metro areas over the weekend \ninto early next week. \n\nOutside of the areas with daily slight rain chances, expect days \nand muggy nights continue to be in near persistence form for the \nrest of the 7 day forecast. A peak beyond that time-frame has a\npotential break from the upper ridge dominance about a week from\nFriday with possible upper troughing developing just west over \nthe Rockies into northern Mexico.",
